<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Władysław of Bytom</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1277–<a href="/years/1352">1352</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Władysław of Koźle</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Władysław of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bytom">Bytom</a> was a Silesian Piast duke who ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kozle">Koźle</a> and Bytom in the first half of the 14th century. Like the other Upper Silesian dukes he accepted the overlordship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-bohemia">John of Bohemia</a> in 1327, bringing his duchies under the Bohemian crown.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Władysław was the second son of Duke Casimir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bytom">Bytom</a> and his wife Helena. He first appears in 1289, when his father paid homage to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wenceslaus-ii-of-bohemia">Wenceslaus II of Bohemia</a>, and in 1303 his father gave him the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kozle">Koźle</a>. When Casimir died in 1312, Władysław kept only Koźle, for reasons that are not known, while the capital, Bytom, went to his younger brother Siemowit. Two brothers in the church received lands too: Bolesław took Toszek and Mieszko Siewierz.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1316 Władysław took over the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bytom">Bytom</a>; how Siemowit was removed is unknown. On 19 February 1327 Władysław, Siemowit and their brother George paid homage to King John of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> at Opawa. Mieszko resigned Siewierz to him in 1328, and Bolesław's death in 1329 let him reunite all his father's lands. Money troubles soon broke them up again. On 21 February 1334 he pledged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kozle">Koźle</a> to his cousin Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leszek-of-raciborz">Leszek of Racibórz</a> for 4,000 pieces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>; in 1337 he sold Siewierz to Duke Casimir I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cieszyn">Cieszyn</a> and gave Toszek to Duke Bolko II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opole">Opole</a>, and around 1340 he made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gliwice">Gliwice</a> a separate duchy for Siemowit.</p><p>During the Polish-Bohemian War of 1345 to 1348 he abandoned his cautious course and sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. He repelled a Bohemian attack in 1345 and on 15 February 1346 signed a treaty with King Casimir III of Poland, who promised military help against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>. Poland did not make use of the opening, and after 1348 Władysław was again an ally of Bohemia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died before 8 September 1352, and his burial place is unknown. He had married first Beatrix, daughter of Otto V the Long, Margrave of Brandenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzwedel">Salzwedel</a>, and then Ludgarda, daughter of Henry II the Lion of Mecklenburg. His only surviving son, Bolesław, inherited his lands. Shortly before his death Władysław had agreed with the Bohemian king that women could succeed in his duchy; this happened in 1355 and led to the division of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bytom">Bytom</a> between the dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olesnica">Oleśnica</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cieszyn">Cieszyn</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Władysław of Bytom” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
